@page "/docs/mcp"
@attribute [Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authorization.AllowAnonymous]
@using ALDevToolbox.Components.Shared
@using ALDevToolbox.Domain.Tools
@inject IHttpContextAccessor Http
@inject NavigationManager Nav

<PageTitle>Connect an AI assistant · AL Dev Toolbox</PageTitle>

@*
    Page archetype 12 (docs / long-form): `.page-head` + `.docs`, a `.prose`
    column and an on-page `.docs__toc`.

    Two things changed beyond the CSS, both because the old shape was in the
    reader's way:

    1. Nine <details> accordions, one per assistant, became a `.pill-tabs`
       strip carrying ?client= - the same move PR 11 made on /login/challenge,
       and for the same reason. Every assistant's instructions were on screen at
       once behind disclosure triangles, so the page read as a wall of
       summaries and the reader had to guess which triangle was theirs. Tabs
       name the choice, and only the chosen one renders. No JS: the tabs are
       links, which is what lets this page stay static SSR and anonymous.

    2. The tool table came from `McpToolCatalog` rather than being typed here.
       It had gone stale - 15 rows against 38 registered tools, two of them
       (`search_snippets`, `get_snippet`) renamed with the Cookbook and no
       longer existing, so this page was telling people to ask for a tool the
       server would refuse. `McpToolCatalogTests` now fails the build if the
       page and the server disagree.

    The server URL is the real one for this deployment rather than a
    YOUR-SERVER placeholder, which is one fewer thing for the reader to work
    out and get wrong.
*@

<div class="page">
    <header class="page-head">
        <div>
            <h1 class="page-head__title">Connect an AI assistant</h1>
            <p class="page-head__sub">
                Let Claude, Cursor, Copilot or another assistant use AL Dev Toolbox on your behalf.
            </p>
        </div>
    </header>

    <div class="docs">
        <div class="docs__main">
            <article class="prose">
                <p class="prose__lead">
                    AL Dev Toolbox can be plugged into AI assistants. Once connected, you
                    can ask the assistant to generate a workspace, look up a procedure on a
                    Business Central release, or fetch one of your saved recipes, and it does
                    the work using your account here.
                </p>

                <div class="note note--info">
                    <span class="note__icon"><Icon Name="info" Width="17" Height="17" /></span>
                    <span class="note__title">The assistant acts as you</span>
                    <span class="note__body">
                        It sees the same templates, the same Object Explorer data and the same
                        recipes your browser session would. Your role and organisation are
                        unchanged, it cannot reach another organisation, and nothing it does
                        escapes the audit log.
                    </span>
                </div>

                <h2 id="mcp-ways">Two ways to connect</h2>
                <p>
                    Which one you use depends on your assistant. Both end up in the same
                    place; the difference is whether you paste a credential or click Allow.
                </p>

                <h3 id="mcp-token">A personal access token</h3>
                <p>
                    A token is a long, random password you create on
                    <a href="/account?section=ai">your account page</a>. You paste it into
                    the assistant's settings or config file, and it uses that on every
                    request. Desktop and command-line assistants work this way.
                </p>
                <p class="prose__cap">
                    If a token leaks, revoke it from the same page and the assistant stops
                    working immediately. Treat tokens like passwords.
                </p>

                <h3 id="mcp-consent">Signing in from the assistant</h3>
                <p>
                    Claude on the web and mobile, and ChatGPT, can connect on their own.
                    There's no token to paste - the assistant shows a permission screen,
                    you click <strong>Allow</strong>, and that's it. Take the access back
                    any time from <a href="/account?section=ai">your account page</a>.
                </p>

                <h2 id="mcp-setup">Set up your assistant</h2>
                <p>Pick yours. The steps below change to match.</p>
            </article>

            @* A <select> in a GET form, which is what the hand-off's own
               "Connect an agent" screen uses for this control - and it had to
               be, once there were nine assistants. `.pill-tabs` was the first
               attempt and it is the wrong component here: `.pill-tab` is a
               fixed-height segmented control built for a handful of short
               labels, and nine names like "Claude on the web" simply overflowed
               the strip. Rendered, it was a stack of clipped half-lines.

               It also sits outside the .prose article on purpose: `.prose a` is
               (0,1,1) and beats a component's own (0,1,0) class, so any control
               left in the prose picks up prose link styling. `.docs__main` is a
               grid, which is what spaces the three blocks.

               No JS: submitting the form is the reader's own click, so this
               page stays static SSR and anonymous. *@
            <form method="get" action="/docs/mcp" class="mcp-client-pick">
                <label class="field">
                    <span class="field__label" for="mcp-client">Your assistant</span>
                    <span class="select-wrap">
                        <select class="select" id="mcp-client" name="client">
                            @foreach (var c in Clients)
                            {
                                <option value="@c.Key" selected="@(c.Key == _client)">@c.Name</option>
                            }
                        </select>
                        <span class="select-wrap__caret">
                            <Icon Name="chevron-down" Width="15" Height="15" />
                        </span>
                    </span>
                </label>
                <button type="submit" class="btn">Show the steps</button>
            </form>

            <article class="prose">
                @{ var client = Clients.First(c => c.Key == _client); }

                <h3>@client.Name</h3>

                @if (client.UsesConsent)
                {
                    <ol>
                        <li>Open @client.Name and go to <strong>@client.SettingsPath</strong>.</li>
                        <li>
                            Enter this as the server URL:
                            <div class="codeblock codeblock--inlinebar">
                                <pre class="codeblock__pre" id="mcp-url">@_serverUrl</pre>
                                <button type="button" class="btn btn--sm copy-btn" data-copy-target="#mcp-url">
                                    <Icon Name="copy" Width="14" Height="14" />
                                    <span data-copy-label>Copy</span>
                                </button>
                            </div>
                        </li>
                        @if (client.Key == "claude-web")
                        {
                            <li>Leave the OAuth Client Secret field empty.</li>
                        }
                        <li>
                            Sign in to AL Dev Toolbox in the window that opens, then click
                            <strong>Allow</strong> on the permission screen.
                        </li>
                        <li>That's it. Ask it to generate a workspace and it'll use the tools.</li>
                    </ol>
                    @if (client.Key == "chatgpt")
                    {
                        <div class="note note--warn">
                            <span class="note__icon"><Icon Name="triangle-alert" Width="17" Height="17" /></span>
                            <span class="note__title">If ChatGPT asks you to reconnect out of the blue</span>
                            <span class="note__body">
                                That's usually a key rotation on their side rather than
                                anything wrong here. Clicking through the same flow gets you
                                back in.
                            </span>
                        </div>
                    }
                }
                else
                {
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            Create a token on
                            <a href="/account?section=ai">your account page</a>. The screen
                            that shows it has the snippet below pre-filled.
                        </li>
                        <li>@((MarkupString)client.SettingsPath)</li>
                        @if (client.Snippet is { } snippet)
                        {
                            <li>
                                Add the toolbox:
                                <div class="codeblock">
                                    <div class="codeblock__bar">
                                        <span class="codeblock__lang">@client.SnippetLang</span>
                                        @if (client.SnippetFile is { } file)
                                        {
                                            <span class="codeblock__name">@file</span>
                                        }
                                        <span class="codeblock__spacer"></span>
                                        <button type="button" class="btn btn--sm copy-btn" data-copy-target="#mcp-snippet">
                                            <Icon Name="copy" Width="14" Height="14" />
                                            <span data-copy-label>Copy</span>
                                        </button>
                                    </div>
                                    <pre class="codeblock__pre" id="mcp-snippet">@snippet(_serverUrl)</pre>
                                </div>
                            </li>
                        }
                        <li>@client.Finish</li>
                    </ol>
                }

                @if (client.Key == "copilot")
                {
                    <div class="note note--warn">
                        <span class="note__icon"><Icon Name="triangle-alert" Width="17" Height="17" /></span>
                        <span class="note__title">If the tool list comes back empty</span>
                        <span class="note__body">
                            <span>
                                Copilot Studio builds a custom connector behind the tool, and its
                                generated definition needs two fixes. Open <strong>Power Apps
                                &rarr; Custom connectors</strong> in the same environment as your
                                agent, edit the AL Dev Toolbox connector, and switch on the
                                <strong>Swagger editor</strong>:
                            </span>
                            <span>
                                Make sure the operation path is <code>/mcp</code> and not
                                <code>/</code> - a path of <code>/</code> posts to the app root
                                and never reaches the server. Then add a <code>produces</code>
                                list with both <code>application/json</code> and
                                <code>text/event-stream</code>, because the server replies over
                                an event stream and the connector has to say it accepts one.
                            </span>
                        </span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="codeblock">
                        <div class="codeblock__bar">
                            <span class="codeblock__lang">yaml</span>
                            <span class="codeblock__spacer"></span>
                            <button type="button" class="btn btn--sm copy-btn" data-copy-target="#mcp-swagger">
                                <Icon Name="copy" Width="14" Height="14" />
                                <span data-copy-label>Copy</span>
                            </button>
                        </div>
                        <pre class="codeblock__pre" id="mcp-swagger">paths:
  /mcp:
    post:
      operationId: InvokeServer
      x-ms-agentic-protocol: mcp-streamable-1.0
      produces:
        - application/json
        - text/event-stream
      responses:
        '200':
          description: Immediate Response</pre>
                    </div>
                    <p class="prose__cap">
                        Neither has a UI toggle. After saving, update the connector, then
                        reconnect the tool in your agent and reload.
                    </p>
                }

                <h2 id="mcp-does">What it can do once it's connected</h2>
                <p>Things you can ask for:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Generate a workspace.</strong> "Make me a new workspace from the CRONUS Customer template, called CRONUS Pilot v2." The toolbox builds the ZIP and hands it back.</li>
                    <li><strong>Generate a standalone extension.</strong> "Spin up an empty extension named Sales Bonus, in the Sales prefix, with App Source Cop on."</li>
                    <li><strong>Find a procedure across Business Central.</strong> "In release 28.1, what calls <code>OnAfterValidateNo</code> on the Sales Line table?"</li>
                    <li><strong>Read part of a codeunit.</strong> "Show me the body of <code>CallPostDocument</code> in <em>Sales-Post</em>."</li>
                    <li><strong>Search your recipes.</strong> "Do we have a recipe for a side-by-side number range setup?"</li>
                </ul>

                <h2 id="mcp-tools">Every tool it can use</h2>
                <p>
                    You don't usually need to name these - the assistant picks the right
                    one for what you asked. The ones marked <strong>Changes something</strong>
                    write; everything else only reads.
                </p>
                @foreach (var group in McpToolCatalog.Groups)
                {
                    <h3 id="@GroupAnchor(group)">@group</h3>
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            @foreach (var tool in McpToolCatalog.InGroup(group))
                            {
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>@tool.Name</code></td>
                                    <td>
                                        @tool.Blurb
                                        @if (tool.Writes)
                                        {
                                            @* &#160; rather than a literal space: Razor trims
                                               leading whitespace inside an element. *@
                                            <strong>&#160;Changes something.</strong>
                                        }
                                    </td>
                                </tr>
                            }
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                }

                <h2 id="mcp-revoke">Taking access back</h2>
                <p>
                    Both live on <a href="/account?section=ai">your account page</a>. Revoking
                    a token makes the assistant start getting "401 Unauthorized" on every
                    request; revoking a connection signs the assistant out and forces it to
                    ask permission again. Neither is a soft delete - the access is gone.
                </p>

                <h2 id="mcp-trouble">If it isn't working</h2>
                <h3>The connector won't add</h3>
                <p>
                    The URL has to end in <code>/mcp</code> and be reachable over HTTPS from
                    outside your network. If this deployment sits behind a VPN, an assistant
                    running in someone else's cloud can't reach it.
                </p>
                <h3>401 Unauthorized</h3>
                <p>
                    The token is missing, mistyped, expired or revoked. Issue a fresh one
                    and update the assistant. For the sign-in flow, revoke the connection
                    and connect again.
                </p>
                <h3>The assistant says it can't see the toolbox</h3>
                <p>
                    Reload it after adding the connection. To check the address itself,
                    open <code>@_serverUrl</code> in your browser - a 401 is the right
                    answer there, not a problem.
                </p>
                <h3>Tool calls come back with validation errors</h3>
                <p>
                    The toolbox returns the same field-by-field errors the web pages show.
                    Tell the assistant which field it got wrong and it will retry.
                </p>
                <hr />
                <p class="prose__cap">
                    Still stuck? Ask your administrator to check the audit log for events on
                    your account.
                </p>
            </article>
        </div>

        <nav class="docs__toc" aria-label="On this page">
            <span class="docs__toc-h">On this page</span>
            <div class="docs__toc-list">
                <a class="toc-link" href="#mcp-ways">Two ways to connect</a>
                <a class="toc-link toc-link--sub" href="#mcp-token">A personal access token</a>
                <a class="toc-link toc-link--sub" href="#mcp-consent">Signing in from the assistant</a>
                <a class="toc-link" href="#mcp-setup">Set up your assistant</a>
                <a class="toc-link" href="#mcp-does">What it can do</a>
                <a class="toc-link" href="#mcp-tools">Every tool it can use</a>
                @foreach (var group in McpToolCatalog.Groups)
                {
                    <a class="toc-link toc-link--sub" href="#@GroupAnchor(group)">@group</a>
                }
                <a class="toc-link" href="#mcp-revoke">Taking access back</a>
                <a class="toc-link" href="#mcp-trouble">If it isn't working</a>
            </div>
        </nav>
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    [SupplyParameterFromQuery(Name = "client")] public string? Client { get; set; }

    private string _client = "claude-web";
    private string _serverUrl = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>
    /// One assistant's setup instructions. <c>UsesConsent</c> splits the two
    /// shapes: click-Allow assistants need a URL and nothing else, everything
    /// else needs a token pasted into a config file.
    /// </summary>
    private sealed record ClientGuide(
        string Key,
        string Name,
        bool UsesConsent,
        string SettingsPath,
        string? SnippetLang = null,
        string? SnippetFile = null,
        Func<string, string>? Snippet = null,
        string Finish = "");

    private static readonly IReadOnlyList<ClientGuide> Clients = new[]
    {
        new ClientGuide("claude-web", "Claude on the web", true, "Settings → Connectors → Add custom connector"),
        new ClientGuide("claude-mobile", "Claude on mobile", true, "Settings → Connectors → Add custom connector"),
        new ClientGuide("chatgpt", "ChatGPT", true, "Settings → Connectors → Add custom connector"),
        new ClientGuide("claude-desktop", "Claude Desktop", false,
            "Open <strong>Settings &rarr; Developer &rarr; Edit Config</strong>.",
            "json", "claude_desktop_config.json", McpServersSnippet,
            "Restart Claude Desktop."),
        new ClientGuide("claude-code", "Claude Code", false,
            "Open a terminal in any project directory.",
            "shell", null, ClaudeCodeSnippet,
            "The toolbox is available in every Claude Code session in that directory."),
        new ClientGuide("cursor", "Cursor", false,
            "Open <strong>Cursor Settings &rarr; MCP &rarr; Add Server</strong>, or edit <code>~/.cursor/mcp.json</code> directly.",
            "json", "~/.cursor/mcp.json", CursorSnippet,
            "Cursor reloads its servers on its own."),
        new ClientGuide("vscode", "VS Code Copilot", false,
            "Open your workspace's <code>.vscode/mcp.json</code>, or your user <code>settings.json</code> under <code>mcp.servers</code>.",
            "json", ".vscode/mcp.json", VsCodeSnippet,
            "Reload the window. Copilot agent mode picks it up."),
        new ClientGuide("copilot", "Microsoft 365 Copilot", false,
            "In Copilot Studio, open an agent and go to <strong>Tools &rarr; Add a tool &rarr; Model Context Protocol</strong>. "
            + "Set the server URL, then under <strong>Authentication</strong> choose <em>API key</em> with the header name "
            + "<code>Authorization</code> and the value <code>Bearer YOUR-TOKEN</code>.",
            Finish: "Save the tool, then publish the agent."),
        new ClientGuide("openwebui", "OpenWebUI", false,
            "Open <strong>Admin Panel &rarr; Settings &rarr; Tools &rarr; MCP Servers &rarr; Add server</strong>, "
            + "or your own <strong>Settings &rarr; Tools</strong> for a personal connection.",
            "json", null, OpenWebUiSnippet,
            "Save. OpenWebUI hot-reloads its servers - pick a model that supports tool calling and ask it to use the toolbox."),
    };

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        _client = Clients.Any(c => c.Key == Client) ? Client! : Clients[0].Key;

        var ctx = Http.HttpContext;
        if (ctx is not null)
        {
            _serverUrl = $"{ctx.Request.Scheme}://{ctx.Request.Host}/mcp";
        }
        else
        {
            var uri = new Uri(Nav.Uri);
            _serverUrl = $"{uri.Scheme}://{uri.Authority}/mcp";
        }
    }

    /// <summary>Stable heading id for a tool group, so the TOC can link to it.</summary>
    private static string GroupAnchor(string group) =>
        "mcp-tools-" + group.ToLowerInvariant().Replace(' ', '-');

    private static string McpServersSnippet(string url) => $$"""
        {
          "mcpServers": {
            "aldevtoolbox": {
              "type": "http",
              "url": "{{url}}",
              "headers": {
                "Authorization": "Bearer YOUR-TOKEN"
              }
            }
          }
        }
        """;

    private static string CursorSnippet(string url) => $$"""
        {
          "mcpServers": {
            "aldevtoolbox": {
              "url": "{{url}}",
              "headers": {
                "Authorization": "Bearer YOUR-TOKEN"
              }
            }
          }
        }
        """;

    private static string VsCodeSnippet(string url) => $$"""
        {
          "servers": {
            "aldevtoolbox": {
              "type": "http",
              "url": "{{url}}",
              "headers": {
                "Authorization": "Bearer YOUR-TOKEN"
              }
            }
          }
        }
        """;

    private static string OpenWebUiSnippet(string url) => $$"""
        {
          "aldevtoolbox": {
            "type": "http",
            "url": "{{url}}",
            "headers": {
              "Authorization": "Bearer YOUR-TOKEN"
            }
          }
        }
        """;

    private static string ClaudeCodeSnippet(string url) =>
        $"claude mcp add aldevtoolbox \\\n  --transport http \\\n  --url {url} \\\n  --header \"Authorization: Bearer YOUR-TOKEN\"";
}
